) refers to this sacrifice—also known as Bahudakṣiṇa and Sarvajit—as being performed by Vājaśravas, the ड़थेर् of the famous नचिकेतस्.
It is a सोम-sacrifice belonging to the एकाह (spread over one day only) type, such as Gosava, Udbhid and व्रात्यस्तोम.
In this sacrifice, the yajamāna or sacrificer has to donate one thousand cows or all his wealth, except the share of his eldest son and land. After this sacrifice he has to live for three days each respectively at the root of an udumbara tree (figtree), in a settlement of niṣādas (hunters), among vaiśyas and with kṣattriyas in the prescribed manner.
